= Web Ontology Language =

== Overview ==
The '''Web Ontology Language''' ('''OWL''') is a W3C family of ontology languages for representing formally defined classes, individuals, properties and axioms on the Semantic Web.

OWL is designed for machine reasoning over [[Ontology|ontologies]] and structured data.

OWL 2 is the modern W3C Recommendation family.

== What OWL represents ==
OWL ontologies can describe:

* classes;
* individuals;
* object properties;
* data properties;
* datatypes;
* equivalence;
* disjointness;
* property characteristics;
* class restrictions;
* identity/difference;
* keys;
* cardinality.

These constructs have formal semantics.

== Classes and individuals ==
A class represents a set/category of individuals under an interpretation.

Example:

<pre>
Class: Planet
Individual: Mars
Mars type Planet
</pre>

Classes are not object-oriented programming classes.

OWL classes describe logical categories rather than implementation templates with methods and mutable state.

== Object properties ==
An object property relates individuals.

Example:

<pre>
Mars orbits Sun
</pre>

OWL can characterize object properties as:
* transitive;
* symmetric;
* asymmetric;
* functional;
* inverse-functional;
* reflexive;
* irreflexive.

These characteristics create logical consequences.

== Data properties ==
Data properties connect individuals to literal values.

Example:

<pre>
Mars hasName "Mars"
</pre>

Datatypes constrain how literal values are interpreted.

== Class expressions ==
OWL can define complex classes.

For example, a class can contain individuals that:
* have some property value;
* have only values from another class;
* have a minimum/maximum number of property values;
* belong to intersections/unions/complements.

These expressions allow definitions beyond simple named hierarchies.

== Equivalence and disjointness ==
OWL can state:

'''Equivalent classes''' — classes have the same extension.

'''Disjoint classes''' — no individual can belong to both.

These are strong logical axioms.

They should not be used merely for approximate conceptual mappings.

== Identity ==
OWL provides <code>owl:sameAs</code> for individual [[Identity|identity]].

It means the two names denote the same individual.

It is not a similarity relation.

This makes misuse especially dangerous in integrated knowledge graphs.

== Open-world assumption ==
OWL is normally interpreted under an open-world assumption.

Not knowing that Alice has a child does not imply Alice has no children.

Likewise, unless a difference is asserted or entailed, two differently named individuals are not automatically known to be different.

== No Unique Name Assumption ==
OWL does not generally assume distinct identifiers denote distinct individuals.

This differs from many programming/database intuitions.

Explicit difference can be stated when required.

== Description logics ==
OWL 2's direct semantics is closely related to the expressive [[Description logic|description logic]] SROIQ.

Description-logic foundations support reasoning tasks such as:
* consistency;
* satisfiability;
* subsumption;
* classification;
* instance checking.

OWL also defines syntaxes, profiles and Web-oriented exchange machinery beyond the abstract DL notation.

== OWL profiles ==
OWL 2 defines profiles optimized for different use cases.

'''OWL 2 EL''' — designed for large ontologies with many classes/properties and polynomial-time reasoning for important tasks.

'''OWL 2 QL''' — designed for query answering over large instance data using relational-style approaches.

'''OWL 2 RL''' — designed for scalable rule-based reasoning.

Profiles trade expressivity for computational behavior.

== OWL and RDF ==
[[Resource Description Framework|RDF]] provides the graph data model and serialization ecosystem.

OWL adds richer ontology semantics.

OWL ontologies can be exchanged as RDF graphs, but OWL semantics is not reducible to treating every OWL-looking triple as ordinary unconstrained RDF.

Different OWL semantic regimes exist.

== OWL and validation ==
OWL is not primarily a closed-world validation language.

A cardinality axiom can lead a reasoner to infer identity or existence relationships rather than simply report missing fields.

Constraint checking commonly uses SHACL or other validation approaches.

== Reasoning ==
OWL reasoners can compute:
* class hierarchy;
* inconsistencies;
* inferred types;
* equivalent classes;
* property consequences.

The results are only as reliable as the ontology axioms and input assertions.

Formal correctness does not guarantee the ontology matches reality.

== Epistemic notes ==
An OWL ontology is an information artifact with formal semantics. It should not be confused with philosophical ontology or treated as automatic proof that its conceptualization is correct.

